WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.645
Hi, I'm Parallax!

00:00:02.645 --> 00:00:04.203
Hesai makes lidar for cars.

00:00:04.203 --> 00:00:08.254
Now it sells a handheld that turns a room into a Gaussian splat.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.602
Photos alone cannot tell where a blank wall is.

00:00:12.602 --> 00:00:17.272
The lidar measures it, poses every camera, and holds each Gaussian on the surface.

00:00:19.817 --> 00:00:22.553
A time of flight lidar measures one point at a time.

00:00:22.973 --> 00:00:24.756
Fire a short pulse and time the echo.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.155
Half the round trip at the speed of light is the range.

00:00:28.575 --> 00:00:30.001
One shot is noisy.

00:00:30.001 --> 00:00:32.140
Millimetres come from averaging many shots.

00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:37.881
The stick carries a lidar, fisheye cameras and an inertial unit,

00:00:37.881 --> 00:00:39.335
synchronised to the microsecond.

00:00:39.755 --> 00:00:43.794
The inertial unit predicts motion between sweeps, so each sweep is deskewed

00:00:43.794 --> 00:00:45.478
and registered against the map.

00:00:45.898 --> 00:00:49.475
That trajectory gives every camera its pose, and the points land

00:00:49.475 --> 00:00:50.776
in one metric frame.

00:00:51.196 --> 00:00:55.317
Training starts from the lidar points and compares rendered depth with the laser's.

00:00:56.675 --> 00:01:00.485
Over a fifteen minute scan of a hundred square metre room, points

00:01:00.485 --> 00:01:02.073
sit under a millimetre apart.

00:01:02.073 --> 00:01:03.660
Density is not the bottleneck.

00:01:05.358 --> 00:01:08.796
Fit appearance alone and a blank wall drifts off its plane.

00:01:08.796 --> 00:01:10.671
Training longer lets it wander further.

00:01:11.091 --> 00:01:14.410
Compare rendered depth with the laser's, and the wall stays flat.

00:01:16.758 --> 00:01:21.009
This is Pixar's plaza, scanned with an early unit and rendered in RenderMan.

00:01:21.429 --> 00:01:25.044
Most of its splats are thin, opaque discs lying on surfaces,

00:01:25.044 --> 00:01:26.358
a few millimetres across.

00:01:27.808 --> 00:01:29.870
So point rate is not the question.

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:33.405
Drift over a building, and whether lidar depth enters the loss, are.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.473
Time the echo and average.

00:01:37.473 --> 00:01:39.361
Lidar and inertia give the poses.

00:01:39.361 --> 00:01:41.563
A depth term keeps the walls flat.

00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:44.162
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:44.162 --> 00:01:44.785
I'm Parallax.

00:01:44.785 --> 00:01:45.097
Bye!
